    <DataNode elementId="AT2G14920-MONOMER" textLabel="brassinosteroid sulfotransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="O82330" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The ST4a protein is a member of the SULT203 family of sulfotransferases |CITS: [15167709]|. Though it was tested against a broad range of substrates including phenolic acids, desulfoglucosinolates and several types of plant hormones, this enzyme specifically acted on brassinosteroids in &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; assays |CITS: [17039368]|. In particular, ST4a works on active brassinosteroids including castasterone, brassinolide, and (&lt;i&gt;22R&lt;/i&gt;,&lt;i&gt;23R&lt;/i&gt;)-28-homobrassinosteroids but not on related mammalian hydroxysteroids or estrogens |CITS: [17039368]|. The C22-hydroxyl group seems like a likely target for the sulfate addition (as depicted in two reactions for this enzyme), but the exact site of modification has not been identified. Transcript analyses indicate that the ST4a gene is expressed primarily in roots, and that its expression can be repressed by cytokinin, indicating an opportunity for hormone crosstalk |CITS: [17039368]|, but the biological relevance of this finding has not been explored. Two similar proteins, ST4b (At1g13420) and St4c (At1g13430) were also tested but showed no &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; activity with brassinosteroids |CITS: [17039368]|. The biological function of this enzyme has not yet been deduced. Given its relative specificity for active brassinosteroids, it is likely to act in the regulation of this important phytohormone. However, sulfated brassinosteorids have not yet been conclusively detected &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; in plants |CITS: [17039368]|. There is some indirect evidence for their existence |CITS: [YOKOTA92]|, but the biological activity of these compounds remains to be determined |CITS: [17039368]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G14920-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G14920.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="O82330" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33687, RXN-16895, RXN-16896, RXN-16899, RXN-16900, RXN-16901, RXN-16902, RXN-16905" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="8" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G14920.1" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G14920" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15167709" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_17039368" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT2G26710-MONOMER" textLabel="Cytochrome P450 734A1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="AT2G26710-MONOMER" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">CYP734A1 (formerly CYP72B1) is a C-26 hydroxylase that inactivates brassionsteroids by converting brassinolide and castasterone to 26-hydroxybrassinolide and 26-hydroxycastasterone, respectively |CITS: [14605216]|. CYP72C1 is a homolog of CYP734A1. Although metabolite analysis of over-expression mutant indicated that brassinolide, castasterone, 6-deoxocastasterone, 3-dehydro-6-deoxoteasterone, 6-deoxotyphasterol and typhasterol are all potential substrates of CYP72C1, no corresponding C-26 hydroxylation products was found. It suggests that CYP72C1, unlike CYP734A1, uses a different mechanism to inactivate the brassinosteroids |CITS: [15773851]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G26710-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="Synonym_1" value="PHYB ACTIVATION-TAGGED SUPPRESSOR 1" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-17988, RXN-17987" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT2G26710.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT2G26710" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_14605216" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_15773851" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT2G36800-MONOMER" textLabel="glucosyltransferase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9ZQ94" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="415" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The UDP glycosyltransferase (UGT) encoded by the &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis UGT73C5&lt;/i&gt; catalyzes the formation of &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosides. The activity of enzyme was tested on a variety of cytokinins. Two interesting characteristics emerged from that study: i) in contrast to cytokinin-&lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glycosyltransferases from other species |CITS:[11331778],[12644686]|, recombinant |FRAME:TAX-3701 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;| UGT73C5 catalyzes the glucosylation of both &lt;i&gt;trans&lt;/i&gt;- and &lt;i&gt;cis&lt;/i&gt;-zeatin |CITS:[15342621]| (albeit with much lesser efficiency that UGT85A1); ii) the &lt;i&gt;N&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation of dihydrozeatin abolished activity of UGT73C5, implying that glucosylation at one site of the cytokinin strongly influences secondary glucosylations |CITS:[15342621]|; and iii) UGT73C5 catalyzes the &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosylation of dihydrozeatin better than other cytokinins and more efficiently than other |FRAME:TAX-3701 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis&lt;/i&gt;| &lt;i&gt;O&lt;/i&gt;-glucosyltransferases |CITS:[15342621]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT2G36800-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT2G36800.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR002213" />
